<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<SharingRules xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <!--
        THIS NOTE LIVES IN A COMMENT AND MUST SIT INSIDE THE ROOT ELEMENT. A comment placed ABOVE
        <SharingRules> breaks `sf` deploy at source conversion with the misleading error "unable to
        find matching parent xml file".

        DECISION D23 (2026-08-09). This file was an EMPTY root element until now; these are the
        first sharing rules on Contract_Review__c. They are the record visibility half of the
        Contract_Review__c grants added to DPEG_Disposition_Edit and DPEG_Disposition_View in the
        same change, and 🔴 NEITHER HALF WORKS ALONE: both permission sets deliberately carry
        viewAllRecords=false, so without these rules a disposition persona holds PSA object CRUD
        and can see ZERO Contract Review rows.

        THEY ARE A DELIBERATE COPY OF THE D17 NDA__c / D20.1 LOI__c SHAPE. Read
        sharingRules/NDA__c.sharingRules-meta.xml and sharingRules/LOI__c.sharingRules-meta.xml
        alongside this file; the reasoning is the same in every particular and is only summarised
        here, with the DIFFERENCES written out in full.

        =================================================================================
        🔴 WHY THE SCOPED SHAPE IS AVAILABLE HERE WHEN IT WAS NOT AT D21
        =================================================================================

        D21 had to fall back to an org wide viewAllRecords on Counter_Offer__c because a criteria
        rule was UNWRITABLE on that object. Contract_Review__c clears the two blockers that
        mattered, and that is why this is a rule rather than a widening:

          1. RECORD TYPES. Counter_Offer__c has none. Contract_Review__c now has TWO,
             objects/Contract_Review__c/recordTypes/Acquisition_PSA and Disposition_PSA, so the
             criterion 'RecordTypeId equals Contract_Review__c.Disposition_PSA' has something to
             name.
          2. A STORED DISCRIMINATOR. Counter_Offer__c had no stored field identifying the deal
             side. Contract_Review__c carries Disposition__c, a stored lookup, in addition to the
             record type itself. Either is a non formula value a criteria rule can key on.

        🔴 THE RULES KEY ON RecordTypeId, NOT ON Disposition__c, AND THE CHOICE IS DELIBERATE.
        Both fields identify a sell side PSA today, but the RECORD TYPE is the field the persona's
        whole surface is already scoped by: the permission sets' recordTypeVisibilities, the Path,
        the picklist value sets and the D20.1 / D17 precedent all key on it. Keying the rule on the
        same fact keeps one discriminator instead of two that could drift apart, and a record type
        cannot be blanked by an unrelated reparent the way a lookup can.

        WHY SCOPING MATTERS ON THIS OBJECT. An ACQUISITION Contract Review is the buy side PSA on a
        live purchase: contract value, execution date and the negotiating position behind it. An
        unscoped grant would repeat, on a third object, the compounding error D17 spent a decision
        unwinding on NDA__c and D20.1 pre empted on LOI__c.

        OWD VERIFIED, NOT ASSUMED. Contract_Review__c is sharingModel Private with enableSharing
        true in the repo (objects/Contract_Review__c/Contract_Review__c.object-meta.xml). A sharing
        rule on a Public Read/Write object would be meaningless, so this was checked before the
        rules were written. ⚠ It was checked in the REPO ONLY and was not read back from the org.

        =================================================================================
        TWO RULES, NOT ONE, AND THE SPLIT IS BY POPULATION - NOT BY ACCESS LEVEL
        =================================================================================

        A sharing rule shares to a GROUP or ROLE and cannot target a permission set, so "Edit
        persona" and "View persona" are not addressable here.

        RULE 1 covers BOTH disposition personas with a single Edit level rule, because OBJECT CRUD
        IS THE CEILING: DPEG_Disposition_View has allowEdit=false, so a View persona holder who is
        also in DPEG_Acquisitions_Team gets READ ONLY regardless of this rule granting Edit. A
        second Read level rule to the same group would be redundant (Salesforce takes the most
        permissive of overlapping rules) and would double the one at a time deploy hazard for no
        behavioural difference.

        RULE 2 exists for a DIFFERENT POPULATION. The Principals hold DPEG_Disposition_View through
        DPEG_Principal_PSG and are not necessarily members of DPEG_Acquisitions_Team. With only
        rule 1 in place they would see zero disposition PSAs, and that failure is SILENT because an
        unshared row is filtered out of a WITH USER_MODE query rather than refused. This is the
        identical gap NDA_Disposition_Principals_R and LOI_Disposition_Principals_R were each
        written to prevent on their own objects.

        WHY DPEG_Acquisitions_Team: every other disposition child object in this repo shares to
        that group (Disposition__c, BOV_Submission__c, Wire__c, Disposition_Offer__c,
        Broker_Listing__c, NDA__c and LOI__c). Using a different group for Contract_Review__c would
        make it the one disposition child with its own population.

        ⚠ CRITERIA BASED, NOT OWNER BASED, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. An owner rule shares by who owns
        the row; the requirement is to share by WHICH KIND of row it is, so that acquisition PSAs
        stay invisible to disposition only personas no matter who owns them. Only a criteria rule
        can express that.

        ⚠ THE RecordTypeId CRITERIA VALUE IS THE FULLY QUALIFIED DEVELOPER NAME
        (Contract_Review__c.Disposition_PSA), not an Id. Ids are org specific and must never be
        hardcoded in metadata. It matches the form used by the two NDA and two LOI rules.

        ⚠ THE RULE NAMES USE "Disp", NOT "Disposition", AND THAT IS A LENGTH CONSTRAINT RATHER THAN
        A STYLE CHOICE. A sharing rule developer name is capped at 40 characters, and
        "Contract_Review_Disposition_Principals_R" is EXACTLY 40. Abbreviating both names keeps the
        pair consistent with each other and safely under the cap; the labels stay unabbreviated.

        =================================================================================
        🔴 PSA_Version__c IS NOT COVERED BY THIS FILE AND HAS NO RULE OF ITS OWN
        =================================================================================

        ESTABLISHED, NOT ASSUMED: objects/PSA_Version__c/fields/Contract_Review__c.field-meta.xml
        is `<type>Lookup</type>` (required true, deleteConstraint Restrict) and PSA_Version__c is
        sharingModel Private with enableSharing true. ONLY a MASTER DETAIL child is
        ControlledByParent. A Private OWD LOOKUP child is shared INDEPENDENTLY of its parent, so
        the rules below do NOT reach PSA versions, and PSA_Version__c needs a mechanism of its own.

        It cannot have a criteria rule today, for the D21 reasons unchanged: it has NO record types
        (deliberate per the Tranche 3 design, which keeps record types on the parent), its only
        candidate discriminator Direction__c carries the token contract copied word for word from
        Counter_Offer__c.Direction__c ('Seller' is the counterparty on BOTH sides), and formula
        fields are not supported in sharing rule criteria.

        🔴 THE CONSEQUENCE IS WORSE THAN AN EMPTY LIST. PsaVersionSelector carries a `SELECT
        COUNT()` WITH USER_MODE whose result PsaVersionService writes back as `priorCount + 1` into
        Contract_Review__c.Latest_Version__c. A user who cannot SEE the prior versions counts ZERO
        priors and rewinds the version counter to 1 on a live negotiation. The three closure
        options are set out in full in the D23 comment block on DPEG_Disposition_Edit; picking one
        is a user decision, as it was at D21.

        =================================================================================
        DEPLOY GATES
        =================================================================================

        🔴 EXPECT THESE TO FAIL CHECK ONLY VALIDATION TODAY, AND THAT IS PHASE TWO, NOT A DEFECT.
        D19.2 recorded exactly this for the NDA pair: check only reported "Picklist value does not
        exist" on RecordTypeId equals NDA__c.Disposition_NDA because the record type did not yet
        exist in the target org. The Contract_Review__c record types are in the same position. THE
        CAUSE IS MOST LIKELY ORDERING BUT IS UNDETERMINED, since a wrong value format produces the
        identical message, and the resolution is the same either way:
          1. deploy the Contract_Review__c record types (and the rest of the tranche) FIRST
          2. then these rules, 🔴 ONE AT A TIME
          3. if a rule still fails once the record type has landed, THE CRITERIA VALUE IS THE ONLY
             THING TO CHANGE; nothing else in this file is in doubt

        🔴 DEPLOY THESE ONE AT A TIME. A batch deploy of sharing rules rolls ALL of them back in
        this org, established in the 2026-07-22 RBAC build. These two join the two NDA and two LOI
        rules in the same phase, so that is SIX separate deploys.

        🔴 GROUP MEMBERSHIP IS NOT DEPLOYABLE METADATA. If DPEG_Acquisitions_Team or Principals is
        empty or wrong in the target org these rules grant NOTHING, and "no PSAs visible" looks
        exactly like "there are no PSAs". Verify as a post deploy gate by OPENING A DISPOSITION PSA
        AS EACH PERSONA, never as an administrator.

        🔴 RECALCULATION IS ASYNCHRONOUS. Criteria based sharing recalculates in the background and
        on an object with existing rows it is not instant. A persona checking immediately after
        deploy may see nothing yet; do not read that as a failed rule until recalculation has
        completed.

        ACQUISITION PSAs ARE UNAFFECTED. DPEG_Acquisition_Edit / _View and DPEG_Acquisitions hold
        their own Contract_Review__c grants with viewAllRecords=true and are untouched by this
        file. These rules only widen access to Disposition_PSA rows; they take nothing away from
        anyone.
    -->
    <sharingCriteriaRules>
        <fullName>Contract_Review_Disp_Team_RW</fullName>
        <accessLevel>Edit</accessLevel>
        <description>D23: shares disposition PSAs to the disposition working team. Covers BOTH the Edit and View personas - object CRUD is the ceiling, so a DPEG_Disposition_View holder gets read only. Mirrors NDA_Disposition_Team_RW and LOI_Disposition_Team_RW.</description>
        <label>Contract Review Disposition Team RW</label>
        <criteriaItems>
            <field>RecordTypeId</field>
            <operation>equals</operation>
            <value>Disposition PSA</value>
        </criteriaItems>
        <sharedTo>
            <group>DPEG_Acquisitions_Team</group>
        </sharedTo>
    </sharingCriteriaRules>
    <sharingCriteriaRules>
        <fullName>Contract_Review_Disp_Principals_R</fullName>
        <accessLevel>Read</accessLevel>
        <description>D23: shares disposition PSAs to the Principals at Read. A separate rule because principals are a different population, not a different access level - they hold DPEG_Disposition_View via the PSG and may not be in the team group.</description>
        <label>Contract Review Disposition Principals R</label>
        <criteriaItems>
            <field>RecordTypeId</field>
            <operation>equals</operation>
            <value>Disposition PSA</value>
        </criteriaItems>
        <sharedTo>
            <group>Principals</group>
        </sharedTo>
    </sharingCriteriaRules>
</SharingRules>
